%[text] ## Field Study: Analysis and tuning of PID controller gains for DC servo drives using Garpinger's trade-off plots
%[text] This field study explores and compares the performance and robustness of two modern tuning methods for PID controllers in motor control: the AMIGO method and the Garpinger method. Both methods are evaluated in cascaded control systems for speed and position control, using Garpinger's Trade-Off Plots to balance performance metrics (e.g., fast disturbance rejection, precise position tracking) with robustness criteria (e.g., maximum sensitivity). 
%[text] The evaluation was conducted on an Arduino-based DC motor system, replicating realistic operating conditions and noise to test controller robustness. The controller parameters were validated by observation of the response times of the speed and position.
%[text] The case study was also published and presented at the Austrian Robotics Workshop 2025 \[3\]. The paper is available online: [https://doi.org/10.34749/3061-0710.2025.3](https://doi.org/10.34749/3061-0710.2025.3).
close all
clear variables
%%
%[text] ### System Identification and Model Extraction
%[text] To capture the behavior of the real system, an open-loop step response was performed using a DC motor from the Makeblock mBot Ranger kit. The motor is driven by a PWM signal ranging from -255 to +255 (12 V max). The measured outputs are angular velocity $y${"editStyle":"visual"} (in rpm) and angular position $y\_{\\mathrm{r}}${"editStyle":"visual"}, obtained via the onboard encoder. The controller operates at a cycle time of $5\\;\\textrm{ms}${"editStyle":"visual"}.
%[text] The step response reveals significant noise in the speed signal, whereas the position response is relatively smooth.
load('MeasurementData.mat')
figure() %[output:59eeefdc]
plot(measurementData.t, measurementData.u, 'Color', [0 0.4470 0.7410], 'LineWidth', 1); hold on; %[output:59eeefdc]
plot(measurementData.t, measurementData.y, 'Color', [0.8500 0.3250 0.0980], 'LineStyle', '-.', 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:59eeefdc]
plot(measurementData.t, measurementData.y_r * pi / 180, 'Color', [0.4660 0.6740 0.1880], 'LineStyle', '--', 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:59eeefdc]
legend('$u$', '$y$ (in rpm)', '$y_\mathrm{r}$ (in rad)', 'Location', 'best', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:59eeefdc]
xlabel('Time (in s)', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:59eeefdc]
%%
%[text] To address this, a first-order low-pass filter with a time constant $T=0\\ldotp 05\\;\\mathrm{s}${"editStyle":"visual"} was applied, chosen to be about four times faster than the system's natural cutoff. This reduces noise while preserving essential dynamics. 
load('MeasurementDataFiltered.mat')
figure() %[output:501a9ea2]
plot(measurementDataFiltered.t, measurementDataFiltered.u, 'Color', [0 0.4470 0.7410], 'LineWidth', 1); hold on; %[output:501a9ea2]
plot(measurementDataFiltered.t, measurementDataFiltered.y, 'Color', [0.8500 0.3250 0.0980], 'LineStyle', '-.', 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:501a9ea2]
plot(measurementDataFiltered.t, measurementDataFiltered.y_hat, 'Color', [0.63529,0.07843,0.18431], 'LineWidth', 2); %[output:501a9ea2]
legend('$u$', '$y$ (in rpm)', '$\hat{y}$ (filtered, in rpm)', 'Location', 'best', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:501a9ea2]
xlabel('Time (in s)', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:501a9ea2]
%%
%[text] The filtered system is modeled as
%[text] $P(s) = \\frac{\\hat{Y}(s)}{U(s)} = \\frac{K}{T \\cdot s + 1} \\cdot e^{-L \\cdot s} = \\frac{2.222}{0.198 \\cdot s + 1} \\cdot \\mathrm{e}^{-0.087 \\cdot s}.$
%[text] This first-order lag plus time delay (FOLPD) model sufficiently captures the motor dynamics and serves as the basis for controller design.
innerLoop.ident.L = 0.087;
innerLoop.ident.T = 0.198;
innerLoop.ident.K = 2.222;
innerLoop.plant = tf(innerLoop.ident.K, [innerLoop.ident.T 1], 'IoDelay', innerLoop.ident.L);
innerLoop.ident.tsim = 0:0.005:2.085;
innerLoop.ident.ysim = step(innerLoop.plant, innerLoop.ident.tsim) * 100;
figure() %[output:7171b007]
plot(measurementDataFiltered.t(find(measurementDataFiltered.u == 150, 1, 'first'):end) - measurementDataFiltered.t(find(measurementDataFiltered.u == 150, 1, 'first')), measurementDataFiltered.y_hat(find(measurementDataFiltered.u == 150, 1, 'first'):end) - measurementDataFiltered.y_hat(1), 'Color', [0.8500 0.3250 0.0980], 'LineStyle', '-.', 'LineWidth', 1);  %[output:7171b007]
hold on; %[output:7171b007]
plot(innerLoop.ident.tsim, innerLoop.ident.ysim, 'Color', [0.00000,0.44700,0.74100], 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:7171b007]
legend('$\hat{y}$ (measured)', '$y$ (modeled)', 'Location', 'best', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:7171b007]
xlabel('Time (in s)', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:7171b007]
%[text] The maximum velocity $v\_\\mathrm{max}$ and acceleration $a\_{\\max}$, needed for trajectory generation, are estimated directly from steady-state $K${"editStyle":"visual"} and maximum input $u${"editStyle":"visual"} as
%[text] $v\_\\mathrm{max} \\approx K \\cdot u\_\\mathrm{max} \\cdot \\frac{2 \\, \\pi}{60} = 2.222 \\cdot 255 \\cdot \\frac{2 \\, \\pi}{60} \\approx 50 \\, \\mathrm{rad/s},$
%[text] $a\_\\mathrm{max} \\approx 2 \\cdot u\_\\mathrm{max} \\cdot \\frac{K}{T} \\cdot \\frac{2\\pi}{60}\\approx 600 \\, \\mathrm{rad/s^2.}$
%%
%[text] ### Garpinger's Trade-Off Plots for Performance and Robustness
%[text] To evaluate the controller design, Garpinger's trade-off plots \[2\] were created for both disturbance rejection and setpoint tracking. These plots visualize the trade-off between performance (measured by $\\mathrm{IAE}$) and robustness (measured by maximum sensitivity $M\_\\mathrm{st}$).
% Controller gains
innerLoop.tradeoff.kp = 2.1;
innerLoop.tradeoff.ki = 13;
innerLoop.tradeoff.kd = 0;
innerLoop.tradeoff.resolutionSpec = "medium"; %[control:dropdown:79e6]{"position":[37,45]}

% Computes stability, maximum robustness and IAE matrices
innerLoop.tradeoff.data = calculateTradeoff(innerLoop.plant, ... %[output:group:784c12e6] %[output:05851416]
    innerLoop.tradeoff.kp, innerLoop.tradeoff.ki, innerLoop.tradeoff.kd, ... %[output:05851416]
    "ResolutionSpec", innerLoop.tradeoff.resolutionSpec); %[output:group:784c12e6] %[output:05851416]
%%
% Visualize Garpinger's trade-off diagrams
innerLoop.tradeoff.numberOfPerformanceLines = 4; %[control:dropdown:1336]{"position":[47,48]}
innerLoop.tradeoff.robustnessLevels = str2num("[1.1, 1.2, 1.4, 1.6, 2.0, 2.5]"); %[control:dropdown:655f]{"position":[47,79]}
innerLoop.tradeoff.upsampleScale = "low"; %[control:dropdown:96da]{"position":[36,41]}
innerLoop.tradeoff.externalFigureWindow = false; %[control:dropdown:34e7]{"position":[43,48]}
innerLoop.tradeoff.smoothParetoFront.method = "movmean"; %[control:dropdown:5aec]{"position":[47,56]}
innerLoop.tradeoff.smoothParetoFront.window = 7; %[control:editfield:7dea]{"position":[47,48]}
[innerLoop.tradeoff.figDistRej, innerLoop.tradeoff.figDistRej] = ... %[output:group:8f114e0e] %[output:7d20ee50] %[output:2d1e5674]
    plotTradeoff(innerLoop.tradeoff.data, ... %[output:7d20ee50] %[output:2d1e5674]
    "NumberOfPerformanceLines", innerLoop.tradeoff.numberOfPerformanceLines, ... %[output:7d20ee50] %[output:2d1e5674]
    "RobustnessLevels", innerLoop.tradeoff.robustnessLevels, ... %[output:7d20ee50] %[output:2d1e5674]
    "UpsampleScale", innerLoop.tradeoff.upsampleScale, ... %[output:7d20ee50] %[output:2d1e5674]
    "ExternalFigureWindow", innerLoop.tradeoff.externalFigureWindow, ... %[output:7d20ee50] %[output:2d1e5674]
    "SmoothParetoFrontMethod", innerLoop.tradeoff.smoothParetoFront.method, ... %[output:7d20ee50] %[output:2d1e5674]
    "SmoothParetoFrontWindow", innerLoop.tradeoff.smoothParetoFront.window); %[output:group:8f114e0e] %[output:7d20ee50] %[output:2d1e5674]
%[text] **1. Pareto Front Analysis**:
%[text] The green and magenta lines represent Pareto fronts for disturbance rejection and setpoint tracking, respectively. Each point on the front offers the best achievable performance for a given robustness level. For $M\_\\mathrm{st} \> 1.2$, the fronts diverge, revealing that both objectives cannot be optimized simultaneously.
%[text] **2. Choice of Robustness Level**:
%[text] A moderate robustness level of $M\_\\mathrm{st} = 1.4$ is selected, as this value provides a reasonable balance between sensitivity to disturbances and robustness against uncertainties. For this $M\_\\mathrm{st}$ value, the controller gains can then be read off the Pareto Front for disturbance rejection (green line) at $K\_\\mathrm{P} \\approx 0.34$ and $K\_\\mathrm{I} \\approx 2.07$ and $K\_\\mathrm{P} \\approx 0.38$ and $K\_\\mathrm{I} \\approx 1.95$ for setpoint tracking.
%[text] **3. Impact of Optimization Choice**:
%[text] Tuning for disturbance rejection leads to better rejection performance but results in overshoot during setpoint changes. Conversely, tuning for setpoint tracking sacrifices disturbance suppression. This reflects the inherent conflict between these objectives in PID control.
%[text] **4. Significance of the Integral Term** $K\_\\mathrm{I}$:
%[text] The results consistently demonstrate the importance of including an integral term $K\_\\mathrm{I} \> 0$ in the controller design. While the literature often suggests that a P-P cascaded control may be sufficient for many applications, the trade-off plots show that an integral term significantly enhances both disturbance rejection and setpoint tracking. By implementing an $I${"editStyle":"visual"} term, the controller achieves superior overall performance compared to purely proportional control strategies. In addition, the disturbance error would not be eliminated with a pure $P${"editStyle":"visual"} control, as the plant does not have a pure $I${"editStyle":"visual"} component.
%[text] By analyzing these plots, designers can select the most suitable controller gains for their specific application, balancing the trade-offs between disturbance rejection, setpoint tracking, and robustness. Furthermore, the findings clearly underscore the practical benefits of including an integral term in the control design.
%%
%[text] ### AMIGO and Garpinger's tuning rules
%[text] The controller gains were initially analyzed using the Garpinger's trade-off plot for disturbance rejection. We now calculate the gains using the AMIGO rule-of-thumb method \[1\] and the parameters $L${"editStyle":"visual"}, $T${"editStyle":"visual"} and $K${"editStyle":"visual"} from our model, which yielded specific values for 
innerLoop.tuning.amigo.kp = (0.15 / innerLoop.ident.K) + (0.35 - innerLoop.ident.L * innerLoop.ident.T/((innerLoop.ident.L + innerLoop.ident.T)^2)) * innerLoop.ident.T/(innerLoop.ident.K * innerLoop.ident.L);
innerLoop.tuning.amigo.Ti = 0.35 * innerLoop.ident.L + (13 * innerLoop.ident.L * innerLoop.ident.T^2) / (innerLoop.ident.T^2 + 12 * innerLoop.ident.L * innerLoop.ident.T + 7 * innerLoop.ident.L^2);
innerLoop.tuning.amigo.ki = innerLoop.tuning.amigo.kp / innerLoop.tuning.amigo.Ti;
disp("kp = " + innerLoop.tuning.amigo.kp + ", ki = " + innerLoop.tuning.amigo.ki + " and Ti = " + innerLoop.tuning.amigo.Ti) %[output:326d3573]
%[text] These values were then compared to the trade-off plot for disturbance rejection. 
figure(innerLoop.tradeoff.figDistRej)
xlim([0 0.7]) %[output:136cd0c5]
ylim([0 4]) %[output:136cd0c5]
hold on %[output:136cd0c5]
pA = plot(innerLoop.tuning.amigo.kp, innerLoop.tuning.amigo.ki, ... %[output:136cd0c5]
    'k*', 'LineWidth', 2.0); %[output:136cd0c5]
legend(pA, {'AMIGO'}, 'Location','northwest', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:136cd0c5]

figure(innerLoop.tradeoff.figDistRej)
xlim([0 0.7]) %[output:136cd0c5]
ylim([0 4]) %[output:136cd0c5]
hold on %[output:136cd0c5]
pA = plot(innerLoop.tuning.amigo.kp, innerLoop.tuning.amigo.ki, ... %[output:136cd0c5]
    'k*', 'LineWidth', 2.0); %[output:136cd0c5]
legend(pA, {'AMIGO'}, 'Location','northwest', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:136cd0c5]
%[text] The results show that the gains obtained from the AMIGO method lie closely on the Pareto front for a robustness level of $M\_\\mathrm{st} \< 1.4$. This demonstrates that the AMIGO method provides optimal controller gains for a fixed robustness criterion of $M\_\\mathrm{st}\~\<\~1.4$, ensuring a balance between disturbance rejection and robustness.  
%[text] However, the AMIGO method has a notable limitation: it does not allow for independent adjustment of the proportional gain $K\_\\mathrm{P}$. To address this issue, the Garpinger method \[2\] was applied. Different values below an $M\_\\mathrm{st}$ value of 1.6 were chosen manually for the proportional gain and the corresponding integral gain $K\_\\mathrm{I}$ is calculated using the Garpinger formula: 
innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.kp = linspace(0.1, 0.45, 10);
innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.ki = (innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.kp + 0.1 * innerLoop.ident.K .* innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.kp.^2) / (0.3 * innerLoop.ident.L + 0.7 * innerLoop.ident.T);
disp("[kp; ki] = "); disp([innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.kp; innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.ki]) %[output:14290647]
%[text] The new $K\_\\mathrm{P}$-$K\_\\mathrm{I}$ pairs lie once again near the Pareto front of the Garpinger trade-off plot for disturbance rejection.
figure(innerLoop.tradeoff.figDistRej)
xlim([0 0.7]) %[output:136cd0c5]
ylim([0 4]) %[output:136cd0c5]
hold on %[output:136cd0c5]
pG = plot(innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.kp, innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.ki, ... %[output:136cd0c5]
    'k.-', 'LineWidth', 2.0); %[output:136cd0c5]
pA = plot(innerLoop.tuning.amigo.kp, innerLoop.tuning.amigo.ki, ... %[output:136cd0c5]
    'k*', 'LineWidth', 2.0); %[output:136cd0c5]
legend([pG pA], {'Garpinger', 'AMIGO'}, ... %[output:136cd0c5]
    'Location','northwest', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:136cd0c5]

figure(innerLoop.tradeoff.figDistRej)
xlim([0 0.7]) %[output:136cd0c5]
ylim([0 4]) %[output:136cd0c5]
hold on %[output:136cd0c5]
innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.plot = plot(innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.kp, innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.ki, 'k.-', 'LineWidth', 2.0); %[output:136cd0c5]
innerLoop.tuning.amigo.plot = plot(innerLoop.tuning.amigo.kp, innerLoop.tuning.amigo.ki, 'k*', 'LineWidth', 2.0); %[output:136cd0c5]
legend([innerLoop.tuning.garpinger.plot innerLoop.tuning.amigo.plot], ... %[output:group:6302d074] %[output:136cd0c5]
    {'Garpinger', 'AMIGO'}, 'Location','northwest', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:group:6302d074] %[output:136cd0c5]
%[text] This result confirms that the Garpinger method not only accommodates adjustments to $K\_\\mathrm{P}$ but also ensures that the recalculated $K\_\\mathrm{I}$ maintains an optimal balance between performance and robustness for disturbance rejection. 
%[text] However, these pairs are located significantly to the left of the Pareto front for setpoint tracking (magenta line), particularly for higher $K\_\\mathrm{P}$ values. This indicates that controllers optimized for disturbance rejection are expected to exhibit considerable overshoot in response to setpoint changes. The results further emphasize the fundamental trade-off between disturbance rejection and setpoint tracking: the system can be optimized for one objective or the other, but not for both simultaneously. Consequently, the choice of controller parameters must carefully consider the specific performance priorities of the application, as optimizing for one criterion will inevitably compromise the other.  
%[text] To validate the calculated controller parameters, the AMIGO and Garpinger methods (with $K\_\\mathrm{P}=0.4$ and $K\_\\mathrm{I} \\approx 2.64$) were tested on the real motor system. The controllers were implemented on the Arduino-based setup, and their performance was evaluated under practical conditions, focusing on setpoint tracking scenarios.  
load('MeasurementDataControlled.mat')
figure() %[output:73e300d5]
hold on; %[output:73e300d5]
plot(measurementDataControlled.garpinger.t, measurementDataControlled.garpinger.u, ... %[output:73e300d5]
    'Color', min([0 0.4470 0.7410]*1.7, 1), 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:73e300d5]
plot(measurementDataControlled.garpinger.t, measurementDataControlled.garpinger.y_hat, ... %[output:73e300d5]
    'Color', min([0.8500 0.3250 0.0980]*1.7, 1), 'LineStyle', '-.', 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:73e300d5]
plot(measurementDataControlled.amigo.t, measurementDataControlled.amigo.u, ... %[output:73e300d5]
    'Color', [0 0.4470 0.7410], 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:73e300d5]
plot(measurementDataControlled.amigo.t, measurementDataControlled.amigo.y_hat, ... %[output:73e300d5]
    'Color', [0.8500 0.3250 0.0980], 'LineStyle', '-.', 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:73e300d5]
plot(measurementDataControlled.amigo.t, measurementDataControlled.referenceSignal, ... %[output:73e300d5]
    'Color', [0.4660 0.6740 0.1880], 'LineStyle', '--', 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:73e300d5]
legend('$u_\mathrm{Garpinger}$', '$\hat{y}_\mathrm{Garpinger}$ (in rpm)', '$u_\mathrm{Amigo}$', '$\hat{y}_\mathrm{Amigo}$ (in rpm)', 'Reference signal (in rpm)', ... %[output:73e300d5]
    'Location', 'best', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:73e300d5]
xlabel('Time (in s)', 'Interpreter', 'latex') %[output:73e300d5]
grid on; %[output:73e300d5]
%[text] The results revealed that the motor followed the desired velocity setpoint accurately, with a significant velocity overshoot for the Garpinger method. This behavior is consistent with the predictions from the trade-off plot for setpoint tracking, which shows that controller parameters optimized for disturbance rejection (with a robustness level of $M\_\\mathrm{st} \\leq 1.4$ tend to exhibit reduced performance in setpoint tracking. Specifically, the controller gains derived from the AMIGO and Garpinger methods prioritize disturbance rejection, which can lead to overshoot during rapid setpoint changes.  
%%
%[text] ### Cascaded control
%[text] In the final stage, a cascaded control loop is realized.
%[text] ![](text:image:3ecb)
%[text] A second PID controller is implemented in the outer position loop. Unlike the inner loop, whose gains were determined via tuning rules, the outer loop gains are directly derived from the Garpinger trade-off plots to optimize setpoint tracking robustness.
%[text] The plant for the outer loop is given as:
%[text] $P\_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\frac{Y\_\\mathrm{r}}{U\_\\mathrm{r}} = \\frac{P\\,C}{1+P\\,C} \\cdot P\_\\mathrm{r},$
%[text] where $P\_\\mathrm{r} = 1/s$ represents the integrator that translates the velocity to a position signal.
%[text] A Garpinger trade-off plot is generated for setpoint tracking. Since the system has an integrating behavior, the plot visualizes the trade-off between the proportional gain $K\_\\mathrm{P}$ and the derivative gain $K\_\\mathrm{D}$, rather than the integral gain $K\_\\mathrm{I}$ used in the previous trade-off analyses. The disturbance still acts at the input of $P${"editStyle":"visual"} and the noise filter applied to the velocity signal was included in the calculation. The resulting Pareto front distinctly demonstrates the benefit of incorporating a derivative action to improve system performance.
% Plant (DC motor with inner control loop)
innerLoop.controller = pid(innerLoop.tuning.amigo.kp, innerLoop.tuning.amigo.ki);
s = tf('s');
outerLoop.plant = 6.05 / s;

% Controller gains
outerLoop.tradeoff.kp = 1; %5;
outerLoop.tradeoff.ki = 0;
outerLoop.tradeoff.kd = 0.4; %1.15;
outerLoop.tradeoff.resolutionSpec = "low"; %[control:dropdown:51b2]{"position":[37,42]}

% Computes stability, maximum robustness and IAE matrices
outerLoop.tradeoff.data = calculateCascadedTradeoff(... %[output:group:26112636] %[output:064e27be]
    innerLoop.plant, outerLoop.plant, innerLoop.controller, ... %[output:064e27be]
    outerLoop.tradeoff.kp, ... %[output:064e27be]
    outerLoop.tradeoff.ki, ... %[output:064e27be]
    outerLoop.tradeoff.kd, ... %[output:064e27be]
    "ResolutionSpec", outerLoop.tradeoff.resolutionSpec); %[output:group:26112636] %[output:064e27be]
%%
% Visualize Garpinger's trade-off plots
outerLoop.tradeoff.numberOfPerformanceLines = 4; %[control:dropdown:21bf]{"position":[47,48]}
outerLoop.tradeoff.robustnessLevels = str2num("[1.1, 1.2, 1.4, 1.6, 2.0, 2.5]"); %[control:dropdown:59a1]{"position":[47,79]}
outerLoop.tradeoff.upsampleScale = "off"; %[control:dropdown:30e1]{"position":[36,41]}
innerLoop.tradeoff.smoothParetoFront.method = "movmean"; %[control:dropdown:8bbf]{"position":[47,56]}
innerLoop.tradeoff.smoothParetoFront.window = 5; %[control:editfield:31d4]{"position":[47,48]}
outerLoop.tradeoff.externalFigureWindow = false; %[control:dropdown:412b]{"position":[43,48]}
[outerLoop.tradeoff.figDistRej, outerLoop.tradeoff.figDistRej] = ... %[output:group:8b0aa2ab] %[output:946be5f5] %[output:033f09dc]
    plotTradeoff(outerLoop.tradeoff.data, ... %[output:946be5f5] %[output:033f09dc]
    "NumberOfPerformanceLines", outerLoop.tradeoff.numberOfPerformanceLines, ... %[output:946be5f5] %[output:033f09dc]
    "RobustnessLevels", outerLoop.tradeoff.robustnessLevels, ... %[output:946be5f5] %[output:033f09dc]
    "UpsampleScale", outerLoop.tradeoff.upsampleScale, ... %[output:946be5f5] %[output:033f09dc]
    "ExternalFigureWindow", outerLoop.tradeoff.externalFigureWindow, ... %[output:946be5f5] %[output:033f09dc]
    "SmoothParetoFrontMethod", innerLoop.tradeoff.smoothParetoFront.method, ... %[output:946be5f5] %[output:033f09dc]
    "SmoothParetoFrontWindow", innerLoop.tradeoff.smoothParetoFront.window); %[output:group:8b0aa2ab] %[output:946be5f5] %[output:033f09dc]
%[text] We again select an $M\_\\mathrm{st}$ value of 1.4 and now determine the controller gains from the trade-off plot with $K\_\\mathrm{P} \\approx 0.62$ and $K\_\\mathrm{D} \\approx 0.2$. 
figure(outerLoop.tradeoff.figDistRej)
hold on %[output:033f09dc]
plot(0.2, 0.62, 'k*', 'LineWidth', 2.0, 'DisplayName', 'Selected controller gains'); %[output:033f09dc]
%%
%[text] The complete control system is now validated using trapezoidal velocity trajectory tracking. The system successfully follows the trapezoidal velocity profile, demonstrating that the controllers are properly tuned. 
load('MeasurementDataCascaded.mat')
figure() %[output:47bc0d76]
hold on; %[output:47bc0d76]
stairs(measurementDataCascaded.outerLoop.t, measurementDataCascaded.referenceSignal, ... %[output:47bc0d76]
    'Color', [0.000 0.447 0.741], 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:47bc0d76]
stairs(measurementDataCascaded.outerLoop.t, measurementDataCascaded.outerLoop.y_r, ... %[output:47bc0d76]
    'Color', [0.494 0.184 0.556], 'LineStyle', '-.', 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:47bc0d76]
stairs(measurementDataCascaded.outerLoop.t, measurementDataCascaded.outerLoop.u_r * 10, ... %[output:47bc0d76]
    'Color', [0.850 0.325 0.098], 'LineStyle', '--', 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:47bc0d76]
stairs(measurementDataCascaded.innerLoop.t, measurementDataCascaded.innerLoop.u * 10, ... %[output:47bc0d76]
    'Color', [0.0 0.325 0.098], 'LineStyle', '--', 'LineWidth', 1); %[output:47bc0d76]
legend('$r$', '$y_\mathrm{r}$', '$10 \cdot u_\mathrm{r}$', '$10 \cdot u$', 'Location', 'best', 'Interpreter', 'latex'); %[output:47bc0d76]
ylim([-2000 6000]) %[output:47bc0d76]
grid on; %[output:47bc0d76]
%[text] The results confirm the importance of tuning the inner and outer loops differently to achieve optimal system performance:  
%[text] - The **inner loop** should be primarily tuned for disturbance rejection, ensuring that speed fluctuations and external disturbances are suppressed effectively. The controller should have an $I${"editStyle":"visual"} component for two reasons. Firstly, the trade-off plot shows that only then the smallest possible $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} value at a certain robustness requirement is met. Secondly, the $I${"editStyle":"visual"} component in the inner loop is required to obtain zero steady-state control error.
%[text] - The **outer loop** should prioritize robustness and, if necessary, setpoint tracking, allowing the overall system to achieve smooth, accurate position control. However, since the setpoint tracking can also be achieved by feedforward, particular attention should be paid to robustness. \
%[text] By synthesizing controller gains directly from the Garpinger Trade-Off Plots, the system achieves a well-balanced trade-off between disturbance rejection, robustness, and setpoint tracking. The combination of optimized PID tuning, and low-pass filtering for noise reduction ensures that the cascaded control system performs with high precision in real-world applications.  
%%
clear pA pG s
%%
%[text] ### References
